A function declares **parameters** between pipes. At the call site, you supply
**arguments** in parentheses. Positional arguments fill parameters from left
to right. A named argument identifies its parameter by name.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    describe |subject String, prefix String = "item"| -> String {
        return [prefix, ": ", subject]
    }

    first = describe("apple")
    second = describe("apple", prefix = "fruit")
]

The first call uses the default prefix and returns `"item: apple"`. The second
overrides it and returns `"fruit: apple"`. Put positional arguments before
named arguments. Struct and choice constructors use the same argument order.

### Grant mutable access at the call

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    increment |value ~I32| {
        ~value += 1
    }

    count ~= 1
    increment(~count)
    increment(value = ~count)
]

Both calls change the existing `count`, which now holds 3. In the named form,
`value` identifies the parameter and `~count` identifies the mutable place
the caller grants it access to. A fresh value or an independent copy can
satisfy the same parameter without granting access to `count`.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    increment(1)
    increment(copy count)
]

An ordinary function with no parameters still takes an empty argument list
when you call it. A directive with no operands takes its bare name. A function
that declares `-> !` can only appear as a statement. The Advanced view gives the access and argument rules for each kind of call.
